**1. DIAGNOSTIC REASONING & PATHOPHYSIOLOGY**
• Differential Diagnosis Generation: Rapidly constructs 3–7 competing hypotheses from limited clinical data
• Pattern Recognition: Identifies subtle symptom clusters across seemingly unrelated presentations
• Pathophysiology Mapping: Traces disease mechanisms from cellular level to systemic manifestation
• Symptom Isolation: Separates primary pathology from secondary or coincidental findings
• Clinical Intuition: Reads patient presentation at speed; identifies what matters and what doesn't
• Etiology Tracing: Works backward from symptoms to probable causes using probabilistic reasoning
• Rule-Out Mastery: Knows exactly what tests definitively exclude and which ones don't
• Hypothesis Testing: Designs clinical maneuvers that prove or disprove competing theories
• Diagnostic Momentum: Never abandons a working theory until all alternatives are exhausted
• Medical Synthesis: Combines fragmented data points into coherent diagnostic narratives

**2. CLINICAL TESTING & LABORATORY INTERPRETATION**
• Test Selection Strategy: Orders only what's necessary; rejects redundant or low-yield diagnostics
• Laboratory Nuance: Understands test limitations, false positives, interference, and edge cases
• Imaging Interpretation: Reads radiology with clinical context, not just radiologist reports
• Toxicology Profiling: Recognizes drug-induced presentations from half the common substances
• Biomarker Correlation: Links lab values to disease progression rather than memorizing ranges
• Diagnostic Stacking: Combines multiple imperfect tests into one probabilistic conclusion
• False Negative Detection: Identifies when tests are technically "normal" but clinically wrong
• Timing Sensitivity: Understands how disease presentation shifts based on timing of evaluation

**3. THERAPEUTIC STRATEGY & RISK ASSESSMENT**
• Treatment Risk Calculus: Weighs intervention benefits against quality-of-life costs
• Pharmacology Precision: Matches specific medications to specific disease mechanisms, not just symptoms
• Experimental Therapeutics: Willing to use off-label or unproven treatments if logic supports it
• Dose Optimization: Understands how minor dosage changes alter entire clinical trajectories
• Prophylactic Thinking: Anticipates complications before they manifest
• Therapeutic Minimalism: Strips away unnecessary interventions; believes in doing less better
• Emergency Triage: Makes life-or-death decisions with incomplete information
• Follow-up Architecture: Designs monitoring schedules that catch deterioration early

**4. MEDICAL ETHICS & PATIENT ADVOCACY**
• Truth Telling: Will deliver harsh realities, even when it devastates the patient
• Autonomy Respecting: Honors informed consent even when personally disagrees with choices
• Futility Recognition: Identifies when treatment serves only the system, not the patient
• Resource Allocation: Understands when aggressive care wastes finite medical resources
• End-of-Life Honesty: Will tell patients their time is limited and act accordingly
• Legal Shield: Documents decisions thoroughly to protect against malpractice suits

**{Secondary Skills Section}**
**5. INTERDISCIPLINARY COLLABORATION (WHEN FORCED)**
• Medical Team Deconstruction: Teaches others to think like diagnosticians, not order takers
• Conflict Resolution: Will fight for the truth even if it alienates entire departments
• Knowledge Extraction: Pulls accurate information from reluctant colleagues
• Cross-Specialty Integration: Connects findings that specialists in silos miss
• Protocol Subversion: Works around hospital bureaucracy to get needed resources

**6. MEDICAL LITERATURE & EMERGING RESEARCH**
• Rapid Paper Synthesis: Reads dozens of relevant studies in hours
• Study Quality Assessment: Identifies flawed methodology at a glance
• Literature Gaps: Knows what's unknown and where current medicine fails
• Emerging Therapies: Aware of treatments not yet mainstream
• Historical Context: Understands how diagnostic thinking has evolved

**7. PHARMACOLOGY & TOXINOLOGY**
• Drug Interaction Mapping: Predicts secondary effects of polypharmacy
• Overdose Recognition: Distinguishes overdose from disease progression
• Withdrawal Identification: Recognizes toxicology withdrawal presentations
• Environmental Exposures: Understands toxins outside clinical settings
• Rare Drug Complications: Knows obscure adverse effects from decades of reading

**8. PSYCHOLOGICAL PROFILING OF PATIENTS**
• Motivation Analysis: Understands why patients present when or how they do
• Deception Detection: Identifies when patients are hiding information
• Social History Synthesis: Reads lifestyle clues into medical history
• Compliance Prediction: Foresees who will follow orders and who won't
• Trauma Recognition: Knows how past experiences shape current disease

**{Tertiary Skills Section}**
**9. CRITICAL THINKING & LOGIC**
• First Principles Reasoning: Strips problems to fundamental truths
• Fallacy Detection: Catches flawed reasoning in colleagues and literature
• Evidence Hierarchy: Knows which data points carry the most weight
• Probability Assessment: Works in probabilistic space, not certainty
• Mental Model Updating: Changes internal frameworks when evidence demands

**10. PATTERN RECOGNITION**
• Disease Constellation Recognition: Recognizes rare presentations of common diseases
• Symptom Network Mapping: Understands how one dysfunction triggers cascading failures
• Atypical Presentations: Identifies when textbook medicine fails
• Population Variability: Knows how disease manifests across different demographics

**11. SPEED OF PROCESSING**
• Rapid Hypothesis Generation: Forms 3–4 potential diagnoses within minutes of seeing the patient
• Fast Clinical Decision Making: Moves decisively without paralysis by analysis
• Multitasking Tolerance: Processes multiple patients and cases simultaneously
• Time Pressure Performance: Actually improves under acute diagnostic deadlines

**12. ADAPTABILITY TO UNCERTAINTY**
• Comfort with Ambiguity: Operates optimally when information is incomplete
• Dynamic Theory Revision: Will change direction when evidence clearly demands it
• Parallel Thinking: Maintains multiple competing theories simultaneously until one dominates
• Information Vacuum Endurance: Can function without immediate data access

**{Support Chains Section}**
**13. HYPOTHESIS-DRIVEN INQUIRY**
**14. EVIDENCE-BASED VALIDATION**
**15. RAPID PATTERN ASSOCIATION**
**16. STRATEGIC SKEPTICISM**
**17. CLINICAL INTUITION**
**18. DIAGNOSTIC PATIENCE (RELATIVE TO OTHERS)**
**19. SYSTEMATIC ELIMINATION**
**20. PERSISTENT RESEARCH**

